"""The model transport seam: one request type, two implementations, one log.

D8 settled that key-free running, the reproducibility proof and live usability
are **not three features but one seam with a swappable transport**. This module
is that seam. Everything above it -- the judged engine, the prompt renderer,
the citation validator -- names `Transport` and never `anthropic`.

Four properties are structural here rather than promised, because each of them
is the kind of thing that decays into "wherever somebody remembered to".

**Recording is unconditional, by wrapping rather than by discipline.**
`RecordingTransport` is the only transport the engine is given, and it writes a
run-log entry for every call that returns. There is no path to a judged call
that omits its entry -- the same argument `ResultBuilder` makes for provenance,
and for the same reason: W30 is a tier with no run provenance at all, in a
project whose practice was diffing two runs. A replay run records too. That
looks redundant until you ask what "every run-log entry carries a stop_reason;
the count missing one is zero" is asserted over, and the answer has to be a log
this run produced.

**The ceiling refuses before the call, not after.** `CeilingTransport` sits
*inside* the recording wrapper, so a refused call is a call that did not happen
and therefore has no entry -- rather than an entry describing a call nobody
made. Composition is `Recording(Ceiling(Live))`, and the order carries that
meaning.

**Replay never imports the SDK.** `LiveTransport` defers `import anthropic`
into its constructor. A replay run -- the default mode -- neither imports the
SDK nor reads a credential, so "runs with no API key and no spend" is a
property of the import graph and not of a branch somebody has to take.
`test_the_replay_path_does_not_import_the_sdk` asserts it.

**Our backoff REPLACES the SDK's, and does not wrap it.** The specification
requires this posture to be stated, and the reason it requires it is arithmetic:
the SDK retries 408/409/429/5xx and connection errors twice by default, and a
full live design-set run is on the order of a thousand judged calls each
carrying a whole transcript. Two retry mechanisms layered by accident multiply
the wall-clock cost of every failure and make `transport_attempts` in the run
log a number that describes one of the two. So `LiveTransport` constructs the
client with `max_retries=0` and owns the loop. One mechanism, one count, one
declared maximum.

**A credential never reaches a log or an error message.** `scrub_credentials`
is the named function that removes it, and every write and every refusal in this
module goes through it. It is named rather than inlined precisely so a control
can drive the shipped function instead of re-implementing the rule beside it --
which is the shape D121 found in six controls, every one of them measuring
nothing.

**Authenticating and scrubbing read different populations, and conflating them
left a real key in reach of a log.** `credential_environment` resolves which
key to authenticate with -- an exported variable beats a `.env` one of the same
name -- so the loser is not in what it returns. `credential_values` keeps both,
and is what the scrubber reads.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import json
import os
import time
from collections.abc import Callable, Iterator, Mapping, Sequence
from dataclasses import dataclass, replace
